
This essay will be a difficult endeavour. Crimmigration is one of the most pressing issues of the current
period. Especially as British politics is becoming increasingly and uncritically pervaded by constant
dehumanising attacks on desperate boat migrants (Gopal, 2023) and the far right surges across Europe
(Sobolik, 2019). But, to the thinking sociologist, migration does not in any sense represent a true crime, but
rather its criminalisation is seen as one of the great moral failures of modern society. With this essay, I intend
not to imply migration should in any universe be seen as a crime, but to show how the criminalisation of
migration has even arisen, and how the societal construct of crime has been weaponised to horrific levels to
further the means and ends of the white supremacist world we live in. I will be using the combination of
critical race theory, labelling theory and strain theory to create a criminological picture of what is happening
with the criminalisation of migration.

Part 1, Labelling Theory

There are a multitude of very specific ideas about labelling theory and it can be relatively broad in what
exactly it means and what it is talking about. For the purposes of this essay, I will be focusing on Becker’s
(Becker, 1963) formation of the theory. In particular, the idea that “deviance is not a quality of the act the
person commits, but rather a consequence of the application by others of rules and sanctions to an
"offender."” (Becker, 1963, pp. 9). Becker’s form of labelling theory thereby says that deviance is not an
inherent quality of action but reactive cultural tendency towards said action.

Labels are not merely descriptive patterns of language therefore but in fact carry a significant hegemonic
baggage across social and legal domains. For migrants, this is immensely relevant. Asylum seekers and
migrants routinely face societal labels that categorise them as “illegals”, “bogus”, “criminal”, “deviant” etc
(Bhatia, 2019). Donald Trump has strategically used imagery of rapists and violent people enough when
using the word “immigrant” such that, in the minds of an electorate flirting with fascism, the word began to
carry those implications even when they were left unspoken. This creation of hegemonic implications within
labels is a fundamental basis for the mass discrimination of migrants. Far right populism functions by

3

blaming the problems of Western Neoliberal economic policy on scapegoats, and immigration is by far the
biggest one (Kirchick, 2019).

Therefore, labelling theory reveals to us how the state and media have constructed categories of
“undesirable” migrants. The process goes beyond classification and is in a sense a cruel inversion of the
traditional concept of self fulfilling prophecies in labelling theory. Often the self fulfilling prophecy occurs
on the side of the labelled, but in this instance it is on the side of the labeller. In this sense, I mean to say that
it is the systemic and absolutely ingrained notion of how evil and scary these immigrants supposedly are that
creates the eternal cycle of treating them like dirt, without consideration of their humanity (Hendry, 2020).
The tragic elements at play here are clear: when migrants are eternally cast as dangerous criminals and
inherent threats to national security, justifying horrible and harsh enforcement measures, the erosion of
migrant liberties becomes much easier.

Forkert at el (2020) have discussed this process at length. They term it “migrantification”, with a Chomskyan
focus on the media’s manufacturing consent role in how migrants are labelled and thereafter treated (Herman
and Chomsky, 1988). In this sense, the process is underscored by displaced people being stripped of their
identities and forced into the label “migrant” as a result of media coverage and societal reaction to the
“deviant” behaviour they supposedly display (how dare people flee from absolute horror). The entire
spectrum of displaced people are all universally reduced simply to “migrant”, with all of its hegemonic
implications of illegitimacy and undesirability. The “migrant”, made to feel like an outsider, cast like an
outsider, then starts to act like one - because by their very label they have been forced into being so.

This progress is exemplified by Sajjad’s (2018) paper “What’s in a name? ‘Refugees’, ‘migrants’ and the
politics of labelling". The social construction and labelling of migrants - precisely Muslim refugees, as
cultural and security threats underpins the process described by Becker whereby deviance is not an inherent
quality of actions but a result of societal impositions upon said actions. As Sajjad points out, this not only
justifies the actions taken against them via the criminalisation of migration, but it also feeds into the overall
discourse of the “European Self” and the “non-European Other”. The creation of a ‘worthiness’ asylum
hierarchy, resulting from this process of “migrantification” (Forkert et al., 2020), ultimately allow Europe to

4

at one end continue to criminalise migration and treat asylums awfully, but also maintain their neoliberal
mythological stance as a so-called beacon of human rights.

Bhatia (2019) points out how, in the UK, immigration offences are constructed as ‘strict liability’” crimes,
which means asylum seekers are pulled into the criminal justice system, despite the fact the manner was once
a civil one. This legal move shows us how the labelling process works in practice, by designating
administrative infractions as criminal acts, the state creates the category of “criminal migrant” where none
existed before. This is a form of differential labelling, whereby the labelling is not applied equally. The
perfect example of this is how welcomed and well treated Ukrainian migrants were after Putin’s invasion
contrasted with how racial minority migrants are treated across the board. This process results in a “deviancy
amplification spiral”. The criminalisation of migration, and its brutal ethics disregarding enforcement creates
an amplification of deviance. As migrants are punished, arrested, deported, and imprisoned, their “criminal”
status is reinforced in the perception of the society that already made has them outsiders. The migrant is not
the alien, they are made so by a society that refuses to look in on itself and question its own massive racism
and xenophobia problems.

Part 2, Strain Theory
Now, having covered the relevance of labelling theory we will turn our attention to Strain/Anomie theory
and its application to the criminalisation of migration.

Perhaps at the centre of strain/anomie theory is the thesis that “crime” occurs when there is a profound and
perhaps even seemingly immutable gap between societal goals and the legally legitimate aims to reach them
(Merton, 1938). When this is applied to migration we can thereby deduce that individuals are seeking better
living conditions, safety or economic sanctuary but face significant legal barriers to migration in the context
of its criminalisation (limited means). Given the dire conditions many migrants are fleeing from, they find
themselves in a situation in which the strain on them forces them to do “criminal” actions - in this case,
seeking asylum in countries who are criminalising these actions (PICUM, 2024). From a Durkheimian point
of view, crime ultimately forms from a problem of structure. In other words, crime is contextualised in the
broader context it exists within. Economic factors, war, education, stratification and more are the direct

5

forces involved in why or why not somebody commits "crime" (Durkheim, 1893). Following this line of
thought, and at the same time keeping the social constructionist nature of crime in our minds, we can
understand that from a Durkheimian perspective, the criminalisation of migration exists within the structural
context of the fleeing from war, economic inequality, political instability and more. In this sense, the cruel
irony of crimmigration is that it on one hand exists in the context of migration being seen as a necessity by
those fleeing horror, but is also vastly demonised and criminalised throughout the reactionary west.

Merton is the architect of strain theory, where I suppose Durkheim was the grandfather. Now, fundamental to
Mertonian strain theorem is the idea that there is a direct relationship between the burdens exerted by
structural factors and the degree to which one is conformist or non-conformist. In other words, the idea that
"crime" is effectively a response to the social situation one finds themselves in (Merton, 1938). Merton
constructs an almost bidirectional-binary to lay the foundations of what he is talking about, that being the
cultural goals/Institutionalised means binary. Cultural goals are things as abstract as personal happiness or
self actualisation or very materially measurable such as economic stability or academic success.
Institutionalised means are essentially the legal means in which one may use to attain these goals. Therefore,
Merton says, "crime" happens when one wishes to attain these goals, but the means to attain them are limited
or nonexistent.

The question we turn to now is where migrants fall in Merton’s famed deviance typology of strain theory.
Understanding this can help us grasp the overall situation a bit better than before. These typologies have two
axes: cultural goals and legitimate means. Merton identifies 5 fundamental typologies: conformity,
innovation, ritualism, retreatism, and rebellion (Merton, 1938). The one we are interested in is “innovation”.
Innovators are those who accept the general cultural goals but use unauthorised means to achieve these goals.
In the context of crimmigration these goals are largely: safety, stability, good living conditions, economic
prospects and more. Migrants use “illegitimate” means to achieve societal goals. The criminalisation of
migration effectively forces migrants into this “deviant” category.

This all overlaps nicely with General Strain Theory developed by Robert Agnew (1992). Agnew is perhaps
the most relevant thinker in the analysis of crimmigration from a strain theory point of view. He identifies

6

three essential classifications of strain that leads to "crime", those are failure to achieve positively valued
goals, removal of positive stimuli and introduction of negative stimuli (Agnew, 1992). In addition, and
relevant here, he also identifies negative emotions as a result of these strains which can lead to actions taken
to correct the cause of the strain (which of course in this case is migration).

Agnew's triad helps shine more light onto our Mertonian analysis. Firstly let's consider failure to achieve
positively valued goals, depending upon the conditions they are seeking to get away from, migrants might
positively value goals such as safety, economic stability and better economic conditions (Refugee Council,
2019). Due to specific circumstances such as war, natural disaster, economic disaster, more or combinations
thereupon, migrants face a sense of failure when it comes to these goals, which is just one part of this strain.
Removal of positive stimuli is also very relevant. Migrants often experience the loss of family members,
homes, wealth, cultural identity and more when faced with particular conditions (Démurger, 2015). Israeli
war tactics in Gaza are an example of this, as much of the country has been wiped out, with homes
completely destroyed and possibly up to 8% of the population dead in the worst war crimes seen in a
generation (Khatib, McKee and Yusuf, 2024). Introduction of negative stimuli is relevant at every state of
the migration process, from making the decision in one's home country to the long and tedious journey of
escape. What I mean to say is, when migrants are on their journey to whatever their desired country is, they
likely face discrimination, xenophobia and in many causes physical hardships during the long journey. The
creation of these negative stimuli, especially at the journey stage, can be overwhelming when combined with
the loss of positive stimuli and the failure to achieve positively valued goals in one's homeland, which might
have originally led to the choice anyway. This all invariably leads to a cocktail of extremely intense negative
emotions such as anger, depression, anxiety and grief - in their totality all of this only leads to more strain
and the determination to migrate to a better country, despite how terrible that country might treat them in the
criminal justice system.

Part 3, Critical Race Theory

Now we arrive at the most essential element of criminological analysis here, that being critical race theory.
Critical race theory (CRT) emerged in the late 1970s (Simba, 2021) and at the core addresses race as a social

7

construct developing from the coloniality of knowledge. As well as the social constructionism of race,
critical race theory also focuses on the issue of systemic racism, detailing how the slavery legacy of Western
countries and centuries of racial categorisation has led to racism becoming embedded in the deepest elements
of society (Banaji, Fiske and Massey, 2021).

Regarding the criminological analysis of crimmigration, CRT has much to offer. Let us start with the concept
of racialisation. Racialisation is the process by which social ideals about race are constructed, given
meaning, and acted upon. In the precise context of migrants, they are often racialised and constructed as
inherently criminal, dangerous or even unhinged (Wilkinson, 2014). This could easily be argued to be the
fundamental process at play in the context of crimmigration. It is the very racialisation of migrants that
paints them as these demonic thugs that are raping all the women and stealing all the jobs (ie Trumpian
nonsense) that completely remove any pretext of morality or ethics in their treatment throughout the
migration process. As this demonisation takes root, it almost becomes a hyperreal social "truth" (Baudrillard,
1981) - something entirely untruth that via its constant social construction/racialisation has become a
collective psychosis - a utter delusion - in the minds of many reactionary civilians, law enforcement and
politicians in the West. The function of white supremacy is the creation of a mass psychosis through the
process of racialisation.
This brings us comfortably onto the construction of "illegality" (Flores and Schachter, 2018). If crime is a
social construction, it is clear that "illegality" is too. This has pretty significant implications. The notions of
the "illegality" of migration, of its "criminal" nature, are not inherent or naturally occurring - they are social
and legal constructs. However, as we know, the criminalisation of migration rests entirely on the increasing
tendency to treat immigration violations as if they are criminal matters rather than civil ones. This
construction works almost in a cyclical fashion. Racialisation and the construction of "illegality" create the
social conditions that allow for the destructive policies of crimmigration. However, on the other end the
resulting policies such as deportation for minor offences, mandatory detention, zero tolerance approaches,
etc, thereby reflect these things backwards, causing an eternal loop. These government reactions to this
constructed "illegality" only serve to further contribute to the societal perception of migrants as inherently
illegal - a brutal cycle that goes both ways. When the migrant arrives, they are already demonised, and when

8

they are brutally punished - the so-called "illegality" is perceived as vindicated. Migration is criminalised
twice - first in its abstract conceptualisation, and second in its enforcement.

A significant element of critical race theory is the focus on institutional racism. When it comes to
immigration enforcement, this again is profoundly significant. Institutional racism is, at its core, the
procedures, policies and practices of institutions fundamentally disadvantaging racial and ethnic minority
groups (Needham et al., 2022). In the context of immigration enforcement, things like racial profiling and
disparities in treatment are common. This element of racism functions at the level of structure, continuously
perpetuating inequality and discrimination through apparently neutral policies that disproportionately impact
marginalised communities. Studies have consistently indicated that non-white migrants are far more likely to
be detained, deported and subjected to harsh conditions (Ryo and Peacock, 2018). Research has also
indicated that immigration detention centres often exhibit profound differential treatment of detainees based
on their race and or ethnicity (Bhui, 2016). What we have found ourselves in, is a situation whereby
migration is being demonised and criminalised throughout the Western world where at the same time those
systems that criminalise these actions continue to have horrific treatment of those who are immigrating.

Bhatia (2019) details extensive and horrible treatment of migrants seeking asylum in Britain in the context of
its increasingly reactionary and criminalising approach to immigration. Bhatia points out that British asylum
processing has becoming a dehumanising process for the racialised “other” whereby asylum seekers are
treated like “global waste” coming through an assembly line. In addition, Bhatia details how the justice
system has systematically failed to correctly process cases, often bypassing essential procedural safeguards
for foreign nationals, which has resulted in more custodial sentences that would ultimately be processed as
community sentences if procedure such as pre-sentence reporting was actually followed. The most damning
result of the criminalisation of migration which Bhatia finds is the horrendous psychological torment and
physical harm asylum seekers face. Denial of medical care, psychological mistreatment, profound disregard
of suicidality and the creation of more trauma was consistent across all interviewees. These abuses and
mistreatments have become invisibilised by the system; totally denied. This mass institutional violence is
done in a detached manner, with the process of adiaphorisation allowing for such systemic horrors (Bowling
and Westenra, 2018). Adiaphorisation is an essential part of upholding the hostile environment created by the

9

criminalisation of migration. It is the process whereby moral objections to the hostile environment are
disabled, being silenced as British asylum migrant policy moves away from even marginally considering the
ethics of its system, to entirely focusing on efficiency and control (Bowling and Westenra, 2018).

Concluding Remarks:

Concluding, the criminalisation of migration, created by mass racialisation and profound reactionary
tendencies, represents an extreme moral failure of the criminal "justice" system. Becker's labelling theory
helps us understand how societal and media driven labelling has demonised migrants, turning them into
eternal outsiders, justifying their harsh treatment. Strain theory has revealed to us the immense structural
pressures forcing migrants into apparent "criminal" acts of migration, painting a vivid image of the extreme
cruelty of criminalising desperate acts of escapism. And finally critical race theory has evinced how the deep
nature of institutional racism continues this cycle, showing how the constructs of "illegality" and "deviance"
are social delusions used to uphold mass discrimination. These collectively come together to help us
understand the deep inhumanity and genuine evil underpinning much of Western civilisation.

